#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:164
msgid "Open the file's web address in a private browser window. If it downloads, assume someone else has a copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:165
msgid "Move it out of the website folder (for example to a \"backups\" folder beside it), or delete it if you have another copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:166
msgid "If it was downloadable and contains wp-config.php or a database, change the database password and the WordPress security keys."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:174
msgid "A copy of wp-config.php may be readable by anyone"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:175
msgid "Copies with endings like .bak or ~ are not run as PHP, so the web server sends them as plain text: database password and security keys included."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:185
msgid "A database dump sits inside the website folder"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:186
msgid "A database dump contains every user, password hash and setting. Inside the website folder it can usually be downloaded by anyone who guesses the name."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:196
msgid "An archive in the site root can be downloaded by anyone"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:196
msgid "An archive in wp-content can be downloaded by anyone"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:197
msgid "The site itself asked for this file over the web and the server said it would send it. Site backups always include wp-config.php, which holds the database password, so assume the contents are exposed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:200
msgid "Move it out of the website folder (for example to a \"backups\" folder beside it), or delete it if you have another copy. Do this first: the web server is sending it right now."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:201
msgid "Open the file's web address again afterwards. It should say \"not found\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:210
msgid "An archive sits in the site root, where it can probably be downloaded"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:210
msgid "An archive sits in wp-content, where it can probably be downloaded"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-exposed-backup-check.php:211
msgid "Site backups always include wp-config.php, which holds the database password. Archives in a web folder are a common, easily guessed target."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-open-basedir-check.php:50
msgid "PHP on this site can open files outside the site"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-open-basedir-check.php:51
msgid "open_basedir is not set, so any code running here (including malware) can read everything the hosting account can read. It is a backstop, not a wall, but its absence is what let one compromised site read its neighbours on a real account."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-open-basedir-check.php:62
msgid "Ask your host to set open_basedir for this site to its own folder plus the system temp folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-open-basedir-check.php:63
msgid "If the host offers per-site system users or PHP pools, prefer that: it is a stronger separation."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of other WordPress installs this site can read. The singular is only used for exactly one, so it has no number.
#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:62
msgid "Another WordPress install can be read by this site"
msgid_plural "%d other WordPress installs can be read by this site"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:65
msgid "These sites run as the same system user, so code running on any one of them can read the others' database passwords and change their files. A break-in on one site becomes a break-in on all of them, and cleaning them one at a time does not work."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:68
msgid "Ask your host whether each site can run as its own system user (or in its own PHP pool), or move important sites to their own hosting account."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:69
msgid "Delete sites you no longer use: abandoned installs are the most common way in, and here they expose every other site."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:70
msgid "Until the sites are separated, treat them as one: if one shows signs of infection, check and clean all of them in the same pass."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/hosting/class-shared-user-check.php:71
msgid "Ask your host to set open_basedir per site if separate users are not possible. It is weaker, but it stops casual cross-site reads."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: administrator login, 2: number of application passwords, 3: number never used
#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:91
#, php-format
msgid "Administrator \"%1$s\" has %2$d application passwords, %3$d never used"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:96
msgid "Several unused application passwords on one administrator look like keys left behind for later. They keep working after the account's password is changed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:99
msgid "Revoke every application password you do not recognise (Users > Edit > Application Passwords)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:100
msgid "If the account itself is unfamiliar, see check C4: demote it and revoke its sessions too."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: administrator login, 2: number of application passwords
#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:110
#, php-format
msgid "Administrator \"%1$s\" has %2$d application password(s)"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:114
msgid "Application passwords let apps and scripts act as this administrator. They are separate from the login password and survive password changes, so keep only the ones you use."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-admin-credentials-check.php:116
msgid "Revoke any you no longer use (Users > Edit > Application Passwords)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:131
msgid "Download a copy for your records if you want one, then delete this archive, and every other copy listed, in the same cleanup pass as the PHP files. Archives first."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:132
msgid "Do not trust a cleanup that removed PHP files but left these archives: the malware rebuilds itself from them within seconds."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:133
msgid "If several sites share one hosting account, check every site for the same file name."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:141
msgid "A randomly named zip archive in uploads could not be inspected"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:142
msgid "Archives with random hex names have been used to rebuild malware after cleanup. This one could not be opened to check for PHP files (the PHP zip extension may be missing)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:144
msgid "Open the archive on your computer and check whether it contains .php files."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:153
msgid "A randomly named zip archive containing PHP code was found"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:153
msgid "A randomly named zip archive sits directly in wp-content"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-archive-seed-check.php:154
msgid "Self-healing malware keeps a zip \"seed\" like this and re-extracts itself from it after every cleanup. Nothing legitimate in WordPress stores hex-named archives at this location."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:197
msgid "A Wordfence firewall loader is set up, but Wordfence is not installed"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:198
msgid "PHP loads this file before every request. Leftovers from an uninstalled plugin are usually harmless, but malware has used familiar names to blend in."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:201
msgid "Open the target file and check that it only loads Wordfence."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:202
msgid "If Wordfence is no longer used, remove the line from the config file shown, then delete the loader."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: plugin folder name
#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:217
#, php-format
msgid "The plugin \"%s\" runs before WordPress on every request"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:220
msgid "Firewall plugins do this on purpose. Anything else doing it can see and change every request, including logins."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: plugin folder name
#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:225
#, php-format
msgid "Confirm that \"%s\" is a firewall or security plugin you installed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:228
msgid "If it is not, remove only this line from the config file shown, then investigate the plugin."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:240
msgid "PHP is told to run an unknown file before (or after) every request"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:241
msgid "This is a classic malware loader: it runs on every page view, before WordPress and any security plugin. Deleting the loaded file alone does not help, because the loader is usually recreated from elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:244
msgid "Remove only the flagged line from each config file shown. Keep any genuine firewall line in the same file."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:245
msgid "Check the same file name at the other locations listed (site root, wp-content, wp-admin, uploads)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:246
msgid "Look at the target file and any hidden file with a similar name (starting with a dot) next to it."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:247
msgid "PHP can cache .user.ini settings for a few minutes, so re-check after about 5 minutes."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-auto-prepend-check.php:248
msgid "If several sites share one hosting account, check all of them at the same time: one infected site can re-infect the others."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: post title, or # followed by the post ID when it has no title
#: src/checks/integrity/class-cloaked-link-check.php:82
#, php-format
msgid "Hidden links were injected into \"%s\""
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-cloaked-link-check.php:85
msgid "An element positioned thousands of pixels off-screen holds links. Visitors never see them, search engines do: this is how paid-backlink operations use hacked sites, and it can get the site penalised."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-cloaked-link-check.php:88
msgid "Edit the page, switch to the code editor and remove the hidden block. Keep a copy first."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-cloaked-link-check.php:89
msgid "Purge any page cache, then load the page with ?nocache=1 added to the address to confirm the links are gone from the real content."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-cloaked-link-check.php:90
msgid "Search your other pages too: this kind of injection usually hits several."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-hidden-user-css-check.php:60
msgid "Custom CSS hides a user from the Users screen"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-hidden-user-css-check.php:61
msgid "There is no ordinary reason to hide a specific user's row. Attackers have used exactly this to keep their administrator account out of sight."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-hidden-user-css-check.php:64
msgid "Look up each hidden user ID (Users > Edit, or ?user_id= in the address bar) and check its role."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-hidden-user-css-check.php:65
msgid "Remove the CSS rule (Appearance > Customize > Additional CSS), then treat the hidden account as in check C4."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:114
msgid "An administrator account looks machine-generated"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:115
msgid "The login is a word plus a random code, and the email address repeats the login. Real attacks created dozens of admins exactly like this, often months before anyone noticed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:124
msgid "An administrator login looks machine-generated"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:124
msgid "An administrator's email address cannot receive mail"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:125
msgid "Make sure you know who this account belongs to. Attackers create administrators with generated names or throwaway addresses so that password-reset emails never reach anyone."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of administrator accounts
#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:181
#, php-format
msgid "%d administrator accounts were created within one hour"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:184
msgid "People rarely create several administrators at once. In a real attack, rogue admins appeared in synchronized waves across many sites."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:186
msgid "Confirm each account belongs to someone you know. Check the other sites you run for accounts created at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:197
msgid "If you do not recognise the account, change its role to Subscriber rather than deleting it: deleting a user forces their posts to be reassigned or removed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:198
msgid "Then revoke its sessions and application passwords (Users > Edit > \"Log out everywhere\" and the Application Passwords section). Changing the password alone does not remove application passwords."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:199
msgid "Set a new random password on it, so the attacker's password stops working."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-rogue-admin-check.php:200
msgid "Check every other site you run for accounts with the same pattern."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:114
msgid "A randomly named PHP file sits at the top of wp-content/cache"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:115
msgid "Caching plugins keep their files in sub-folders. A hex-named PHP file here matches a known malware bootstrap."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:117
msgid "Check which caching plugin, if any, created it. If none, treat it as part of an infection."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:171
msgid "A must-use plugin named like a known self-reinstalling backdoor"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:172
msgid "Files named wp-<random hex>.php in mu-plugins were used as a \"recovery channel\" that reinstalls a spam operation every 30 minutes. Must-use plugins run on every request and never appear as active plugins."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:175
msgid "Check every site on the same hosting account for the same pattern at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:176
msgid "Removing this file alone is not enough: also look for the plugin it reinstalls and for database settings it left behind."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:191
msgid "A must-use plugin contains code patterns typical of malware"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:192
msgid "Must-use plugins run on every request before normal plugins. The patterns listed are how malware hides what it does."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:194
msgid "Compare the file with a known-good copy from whoever installed it. If you do not know who installed it, treat the site as compromised."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:203
msgid "A must-use plugin with no name"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: plugin name from the file header
#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:205
#, php-format
msgid "Must-use plugin: %s"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:208
msgid "Must-use plugins run on every request and cannot be switched off from the dashboard. Hosts and developers add them on purpose; malware does too. Confirm you know where this one came from."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:210
#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:327
msgid "If you recognise it, acknowledge it so Earlyward only alerts when it changes."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: drop-in file name
#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:235
#, php-format
msgid "The %s drop-in contains code patterns typical of malware"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:238
msgid "WordPress loads drop-ins automatically. Fake caching and database drop-ins are a known hiding place."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:240
msgid "Reinstall the drop-in from the plugin that owns it (usually by turning its caching or database feature off and on), or remove it if nothing should own it."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:266
msgid "A leftover maintenance page sits in wp-content"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:267
msgid "WordPress only loads maintenance.php while it is updating itself, and this file looks like an automatically generated maintenance page from a plugin that is no longer installed. It is harmless on its own, but a file nobody owns is worth removing."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:269
msgid "If you removed a maintenance-mode plugin, delete wp-content/maintenance.php. If you did not, find out how it got there."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: drop-in file name
#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:280
#, php-format
msgid "The %s drop-in does not belong to any installed plugin"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:283
msgid "WordPress loads this file automatically. Normally it is written by a caching or database plugin and names that plugin. Malware has used fake drop-ins with these exact names."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:285
msgid "Find out which plugin created it. If it belongs to a plugin you removed, delete the drop-in; if you cannot tell, compare it with a fresh copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:313
msgid "Suspicious PHP file directly in wp-content"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:314
msgid "WordPress never puts randomly named PHP files here. This matches the loader of a known self-healing malware kit."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:316
msgid "Check whether a .user.ini or .htaccess points at this file (see check C1), and look for a hidden twin starting with a dot."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:324
msgid "A PHP file sits directly in wp-content"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:325
msgid "Some plugins put configuration files here (for example cache settings). Confirm you know where it came from."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:345
msgid "A hidden PHP file (name starting with a dot)"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:346
msgid "Files starting with a dot are hidden from most file listings. WordPress core never ships one; malware uses them for its bootstrap and for background processes."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:349
msgid "Check running processes for anything executing this path (your host can do this): a process can keep running after the file is deleted."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/integrity/class-unexpected-php-check.php:350
msgid "Look for a non-hidden file with the same name next to it."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: name of the tool that switched the code off.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:79
#, php-format
msgid "%s switched off custom code after a crash"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:82
msgid "While this marker exists, the code it manages does not run at all, but the site keeps answering normally. Pages can lose their styling, features and custom addresses without any error being shown."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:97
msgid "Open the file named in the evidence and check it for a real error (run it in a test copy if you can). A crash can also be a one-off, such as the host killing a long request."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:98
msgid "Once the file is fixed, or you are sure it was a one-off, delete the marker file to switch the code back on."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:99
msgid "Purge page caches afterwards, then check the homepage (check A1)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:112
msgid "WordPress caught a fatal error recently"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:113
msgid "WordPress emails a \"recovery mode\" link when a plugin or theme causes a fatal error. Visitors may have seen \"There has been a critical error on this website\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:124
msgid "Look for the recovery-mode email sent to the admin address at that time; it names the plugin or theme."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-crash-check.php:125
msgid "Check the error log (A3) around that time for the \"PHP Fatal error\" line."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:80
msgid "DISABLE_WP_CRON is set, so WordPress expects a system cron job to call wp-cron.php, and that is not happening. Updates, scheduled posts, backups and these scans have stopped."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:81
msgid "Scheduled jobs are running late. WordPress only runs them when the site gets visits (or a system cron job calls wp-cron.php), and something is stopping that: very low traffic, a page cache that serves every visit, or a firewall blocking the server's request to itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:84
msgid "Ask your host (or check the control panel) for a cron job that requests wp-cron.php every 5-15 minutes, and add one if it is missing."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:85
msgid "Or remove DISABLE_WP_CRON from wp-config.php to go back to visit-triggered cron."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:88
msgid "Set up a system cron job that requests your-site/wp-cron.php every 5-15 minutes, then add define( 'DISABLE_WP_CRON', true ); to wp-config.php."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:89
msgid "If the site has visitors, check that your firewall allows the server to reach its own address (WordPress Site Health shows this as a loopback error)."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of hours.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-cron-check.php:99
#, php-format
msgid "Scheduled jobs are %d hours overdue (WP-Cron is not running)"
msgid_plural "Scheduled jobs are %d hours overdue (WP-Cron is not running)"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#. translators: 1: log file path, 2: file size such as 1.5 GB.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:127
#, php-format
msgid "Error log %1$s is %2$s"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: log file path, 2: growth per day such as 60.0 MB.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:133
#, php-format
msgid "Error log %1$s is growing by about %2$s a day"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:137
msgid "A runaway log can fill the hosting account's disk quota, which stops uploads, updates, email and sometimes every site on the account. It usually comes from one warning repeated on every page view."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:140
msgid "Fix the cause first: the most frequent recent line is shown as evidence. It usually names the plugin or theme file."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:141
msgid "Then empty or delete the log file (keep a copy of the end of it if you need it)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-error-log-check.php:142
msgid "Do not rely on setting error_reporting in wp-config.php: WordPress resets the level later during start-up, so it silently does nothing. Fix the warning, or set the level in a must-use plugin."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:95
msgid "The site could not load its own homepage"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:96
msgid "Earlyward checks the homepage from the server itself. Some hosts or firewalls block that, which is harmless; but it also happens when the site is down."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:99
msgid "Open the site in a browser. If it works, ask your host whether requests from the server to itself are blocked (WordPress Site Health shows the same \"loopback\" problem)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:100
msgid "If it does not work, check the error log (see check A3)."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: HTTP status code.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:112
#, php-format
msgid "The homepage returns an error (HTTP %d)"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:115
msgid "Visitors see an error page."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:117
msgid "Check the error log (check A3) and any recent plugin, theme or code change."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: HTTP status code.
#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:128
#, php-format
msgid "The homepage refused the server's own request (HTTP %d)"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:131
msgid "A firewall or security rule may be blocking requests from the server itself, or the homepage really is unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:133
msgid "Open the site in a private browser window. If it works, the block only affects the server's own requests; ask your host to allow them."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:144
msgid "The homepage answers \"OK\" but is nearly empty"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:145
msgid "An empty or non-HTML page with a success code usually means a fatal error, a firewall challenge page, or a broken cache. Uptime monitors that only check the status code will not notice."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:147
msgid "Open the homepage in a private window and look at what visitors get. Check the error log (A3)."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:224
msgid "The homepage loads, but parts that worked before are missing"
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:225
msgid "The page still returns \"OK\", so ordinary uptime checks stay green. Missing styles, menus or API routes usually mean some code stopped loading after an error, which is how a real site stayed broken for 12 days unnoticed."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:228
msgid "Open the homepage in a private window and compare it with how it should look."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:229
msgid "Check the error log (A3) and crash markers (A2) for the code that owns the missing parts."
msgstr ""

#: src/checks/runtime/class-outage-check.php:230
msgid "If you changed the site on purpose, record the new healthy state: wp earlyward baseline --reset"
msgstr ""

